 "case:accusation.successTitle": "Case closed",
 "case:briefing.0": "Hackney, East London. Monday 11 March 2024, nine o’clock in the morning.",
 "case:briefing.1": "Last Thursday night Jonah Pryce — thirty-four, 1.3 million followers, the most feared food critic on the internet — ordered a takeaway from a restaurant he had torn apart in January. He had a severe peanut allergy, and said so on the order. At 21:29 he posted himself taking the first spoonful. By 22:19 he was dead.",
 "case:briefing.2": "A lab found peanut in the curry. Thambi’s chef-owner, Priya Ratnam, has been arrested on suspicion of manslaughter, her restaurant is shut, and half the internet has already convicted her. She swears her kitchen is nut-free.",
 "case:briefing.3": "Her solicitors have hired you to find out what really happened. Jonah’s fiancée has given you the code to the flat. Everyone involved, she says, has been lying to the police about something.",
 "case:briefing.4": "Find out who killed Jonah Pryce, how it was done and why — and be ready to prove it.",
 "case:epilogue.0": "Marcus Doyle said nothing at all, then or later, on the advice of the solicitor he had asked for. He didn’t need to. Jonah’s pens in the family sharps bin, the sachet missing from his son’s box and the run his daughter had made for him were enough, and he was charged with murder.",
 "case:epilogue.1": "For a year he had used Jonah’s name to shake down restaurants — thirty-one of them, about forty thousand pounds — and steered Jonah towards the ones that wouldn’t pay. When Jonah confronted him at Sunday lunch, Marcus had eight days before the video went out. He chose Thambi because Priya had refused him, and nine o’clock because he knew exactly how long his brother-in-law spent in the shower before he filmed.",
 "case:epilogue.2": "He borrowed his daughter for an alibi, his son’s medicine for a weapon, and the old pens his wife kept to teach people how to save a child’s life. He meant to drop Jonah’s pens in the canal on the way home, but a man was smoking on the deck of a narrowboat, so he took them home, to the one bin in the house nobody ever opens. And he hadn’t counted on a fifteen-year-old running faster than he ever had.",
 "case:epilogue.3": "At nine o’clock on Monday 18 March, Lena posted the video Jonah never got to post. RECEIPTS had eleven million views by the evening. By the end of the week, thirty-one restaurants had come forward.",
 "case:epilogue.4": "The case against Priya Ratnam was dropped. Thambi reopened in April with a queue round the block and a new jar of chilli oil: ‘Peanut-free. I rang the factory myself.’ Mihai got an account of his own. Owen moved to Walthamstow, and Lena went home to Ljubljana.",
 "case:epilogue.5": "Hannah Doyle took the box of old pens to the pharmacy to be destroyed, and taught her next training session with a dummy pen. Evie’s 7.2 kilometres are still on her father’s profile: the fastest run he never ran.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.0": "Review your board. Who was in Flat 3 at 21:19, where did the poison and the old pens come from, and who would be ruined on Monday?",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.1": "Every part of the accusation needs a card: the alibi, the means and the motive. If one is missing, the hints above will have pointed you to it. Re-read your notebook.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.2": "The killer is Marcus Doyle: peanut immunotherapy powder on the curry and useless pens in Jonah’s pouch, to stop Jonah exposing his extortion of restaurants. Prove it with your run deduction, your medicine deduction, and your shakedown deduction.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.0": "You may already have enough for an arrest. Whose alibi has fallen apart, whose house did the weapons come from, and who needed Jonah silent before Monday?",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.1": "Only one person had the guest code, the medicine, the old pens and no alibi, and stood to be ruined at nine o’clock on Monday.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.2": "Accuse Marcus Doyle: he sprinkled peanut immunotherapy powder onto the curry and swapped his pens for useless ones, to stop Jonah exposing his extortion of restaurants. Prove it with your run deduction (or the family chat), your medicine deduction (or Alfie’s medicine box), and your shakedown deduction (or the draft video RECEIPTS).",
 "case:hints.h_chat.levels.0": "Marcus says his run proves where he was. Does his family agree?",
 "case:hints.h_chat.levels.1": "The Doyles keep an iPad on the kitchen counter.",
 "case:hints.h_chat.levels.2": "In the Doyles’ House, examine the iPad on the counter.",
 "case:hints.h_hannah.levels.0": "The medicine came from the Doyles’ house, and Hannah knows the guest code. Could she have used it?",
 "case:hints.h_hannah.levels.1": "Ask Hannah what she was doing on Thursday, and look at her desk.",
 "case:hints.h_hannah.levels.2": "Answer ‘No — she was tutoring on camera from 21:00 to 21:45’, with Hannah’s tutoring record and your ‘Five minutes in the shower’ deduction.",
 "case:hints.h_hannah_row.levels.0": "Hannah says her husband and her brother never had a cross word.",
 "case:hints.h_hannah_row.levels.1": "Jonah’s laptop tells a different story.",
 "case:hints.h_hannah_row.levels.2": "Challenge Hannah’s ‘got on like brothers’ statement with the messages between Jonah and Marcus.",
 "case:hints.h_jacket.levels.0": "Jonah’s new pens didn’t vanish. Somebody took them away.",
